# Enemy AI System for Pimpa-Raka

# This rule documents the enemy AI architecture for creating and extending enemy behaviors.
# Reference: scripts/enemies/README_ENEMY_AI.md for complete documentation.

# ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
# ====================
# The enemy system uses a state machine with vision-based detection and personality parameters.
#
# Enemy.gd (CharacterBody2D)
# ├── Vision System (line-of-sight, vision cones)
# ├── Awareness System (buildup/decay, last known position)
# ├── Personality System (aggression, caution, persistence)
# └── StateMachine
#     ├── idle (patrol)
#     ├── suspicious (investigating)
#     ├── chase (pursuing player)
#     ├── attack (pluggable - LungeAttackState or EnemyAttackState)
#     ├── search (looking for lost player)
#     └── hurt (stagger reaction)

# STATE TRANSITIONS
# =================
# idle → suspicious: Peripheral vision detects player
# idle → chase: Direct vision sees player
# suspicious → chase: Found player or awareness threshold reached
# suspicious → idle: Timeout with no evidence
# chase → attack: Player on same level and in attack trigger range
# chase → search: Lost line of sight
# attack → chase: Attack complete, player moved away
# search → chase: Found player again
# search → idle: Search timeout
# Any state → hurt: Took damage
# hurt → chase: Recovery complete (enemy becomes angry)

# ATTACK STATES (PLUGGABLE)
# =========================
# The attack node under StateMachine can use different attack scripts:
#
# LungeAttackState.gd (Recommended):
#   - Enemy JUMPS toward player
#   - Configurable: lunge_speed_x, lunge_jump_force
#   - Best for aggressive melee enemies
#
# EnemyAttackState.gd (Simple):
#   - Enemy MOVES forward during strike
#   - No jumping, basic approach
#   - Good for basic patrol enemies

# ATTACK TRIGGER
# ==============
# Attack triggers when:
#   distance_to_player <= attack_range * attack_trigger_range_multiplier
#
# Set attack_trigger_range_multiplier high (e.g., 6.0) for aggressive lunging

# VISION SYSTEM
# =============
# Direct Vision:
#   - Range: vision_range (default 120px)
#   - Angle: vision_angle (default 60° half-angle)
#   - Blocked by terrain (raycast check)
#   - Triggers immediate alert
#
# Peripheral Vision:
#   - Range: peripheral_range (default 60px)
#   - Angle: peripheral_angle (default 120° half-angle)
#   - Triggers suspicion, not immediate chase

# AWARENESS SYSTEM
# ================
# awareness_level: 0.0 (unaware) to 2.0 (hyper-alert)
#   - 0.0-0.5: Suspicious, looking around
#   - 0.5-1.0: Alert, actively investigating
#   - 1.0+: Combat mode
#   - 2.0: After being hit (maximum alertness)
#
# last_known_player_position: Updated when player seen, used for search behavior

# PERSONALITY SYSTEM
# ==================
# Three parameters (0.0 to 1.0) affect enemy behavior:
#
# aggression (default 0.5):
#   - Higher: Faster chase, shorter attack telegraph, more aggressive
#   - Lower: Slower, longer warning before attacks
#
# caution (default 0.5):
#   - Higher: Shorter effective vision range
#   - Lower: Longer vision range
#
# persistence (default 0.5):
#   - Higher: Searches longer, awareness decays slower
#   - Lower: Gives up quickly, awareness decays fast

# CREATING NEW ENEMY TYPES
# ========================
# 1. Duplicate scenes/enemies/Enemy2.tscn
# 2. Rename root node to your enemy name
# 3. Create new SpriteFrames resource with animations: idle, walk, attack, dead
# 4. Adjust exported parameters in Inspector
# 5. Choose attack state (LungeAttackState or EnemyAttackState)

# PRESET CONFIGURATIONS
# =====================
# Hopper (Current Default - Enemy2):
#   attack_trigger_range_multiplier: 6.0, aggression: 0.7
#   Attack: LungeAttackState
#
# Rusher (Fast):
#   speed: 30, vision_range: 80, aggression: 0.9, caution: 0.1, persistence: 0.3
#
# Stalker (Cautious):
#   speed: 14, vision_range: 160, aggression: 0.3, caution: 0.8, persistence: 0.7
#
# Brute (Heavy):
#   speed: 12, attack_damage: 2, max_health: 5, aggression: 0.7, persistence: 0.9

# REQUIRED ANIMATIONS
# ===================
# Each enemy's SpriteFrames must include:
#   - idle: Standing/patrolling
#   - walk: Moving/chasing
#   - attack: Attack animation
#   - dead: Death animation (plays once, then enemy removed)
#   - hurt: (Optional) Damage reaction

# FILE REFERENCE
# ==============
# scenes/enemies/Enemy2.tscn: Current enemy scene (hopper with lunge attack)
#
# scripts/enemies/Enemy.gd: Main class with vision, awareness, personality
# scripts/enemies/EnemyIdleState.gd: Patrol behavior
# scripts/enemies/EnemySuspiciousState.gd: Investigation after glimpse
# scripts/enemies/EnemyChaseState.gd: Active pursuit with smart direction
# scripts/enemies/LungeAttackState.gd: Lunge/jump attack (telegraph → lunge → recovery)
# scripts/enemies/EnemyAttackState.gd: Simple forward attack (telegraph → strike → recovery)
# scripts/enemies/EnemySearchState.gd: Last known position search
# scripts/enemies/EnemyHurtState.gd: Damage stagger
# scripts/enemies/EnemyMovementComponent.gd: Movement interface
# scripts/enemies/README_ENEMY_AI.md: Complete documentation
#
# resources/characters/enemy2_sprite_frames.tres: Sprite frames for Enemy2

# NAMING CONVENTIONS
# ==================
# States: {Behavior}State or Enemy{Behavior}State (e.g., LungeAttackState, EnemyChaseState)
# Enemy scenes: {Name}.tscn or Enemy{Number}.tscn
# Sprite frames: {name}_sprite_frames.tres

# LADDER HANDLING
# ===============
# Enemies automatically:
#   - Walk over ladder tiles during patrol (don't turn around)
#   - Move toward player when on ladder (don't block access)
#   - Keep chasing through ladder areas

# INTEGRATION NOTES
# =================
# - Enemies use the same State/StateMachine pattern as Player
# - Stomp kills handled in Enemy._check_player_collision()
# - Damage dealt via Enemy._damage_player() which notifies HealthManager
# - Death animation plays before queue_free() (handled in Enemy.die())
# - Physics priority: Enemy runs after Player (priority 100)
# - State machine runs before Enemy (priority 50)
